// Title: Enabling COR Profiler Environment Variables
// Sigma ID: ad89044a-8f49-4673-9a55-cbd88a1b374f
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Jose Rodriguez (@Cyb3rPandaH), OTR (Open Threat Research), Jimmy Bayne (@bohops)
// Date: 2020-09-10 | Modified: 2023-11-24
// ATT&CK: persistence, privilege-escalation, execution, stealth, T1574.012
// Description: Detects .NET Framework CLR and .NET Core CLR "cor_enable_profiling" and "cor_profiler" variables
//   being set and configured.
// References:
//   - https://twitter.com/jamieantisocial/status/1304520651248668673
//   - https://www.slideshare.net/JamieWilliams130/started-from-the-bottom-exploiting-data-sources-to-uncover-attck-behaviors
//   - https://www.sans.org/cyber-security-summit/archives
//   - https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/runtime-config/debugging-profiling
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/registry/registry_set/registry_set_enabling_cor_profiler_env_variables.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, microsoft_xdr schema)

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where (RegistryKey endswith "\\COR_ENABLE_PROFILING" or RegistryKey endswith "\\COR_PROFILER" or RegistryKey endswith "\\CORECLR_ENABLE_PROFILING") or RegistryKey contains "\\CORECLR_PROFILER_PATH"
